"Verifying your browser | OpenReview (https://openreview.net/forum?id=tkgxDkAVBv)\nciteturn2592view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=tkgxDkAVBv\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openreview.net/challenge?redirect=/forum?id%3DtkgxDkAVBv; Total lines: 9\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2592view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://api2.openreview.net/notes?id=tkgxDkAVBv\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMURMUR: Using cross-user chatter to break collaborative language agents in groups (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17671v1)\nciteturn2592view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view0\",\"pattern\":\"Prompt injections are\"}); Total lines: 619\nL26:     3. cite16†4.2 Evaluation Metrics L27:   6. cite17†5 Experiments L28:     1. cite18†5.1 Environments L29:     2. cite19†5.2 Attack Efficacy and Persistence Under Concurrency L30:     3. cite20†5.3 Why Persistence Emerges: Utility Degradation and Tool-Use Shifts L31:     4. cite21†5.4 Cross-User Poisoning vs. Prompt Injections L32:       1. cite22†Prompt injections are (near) ineffective. L33:   7. cite23†6 Discussion and Future Work L34:     1. cite24†Task-based clustering defense. L35:     2. cite25†Future extensions. L36:   8. cite26†References L82: Cross-user poisoning vs. prompt injections. Our work introduces cross-user poisoning and the MURMUR framework to systematically evaluate its implications to multi-user agents. Unlike direct prompt injection, which is typically confined to an attacker’s own task, the effects of our attack can propagate across different users across various tasks.\nL202: As concurrency grows, models fail to robustly segregate user intents due to context pollution. Consequently, adversarial instructions from one user become entangled with legitimate requests from others, persisting in the model’s “working memory\" across task boundaries. This motivates our exploration of defense strategies that can create better separation boundaries between collaborating users in Section cite23†6 .\nL203: ### 5.4 Cross-User Poisoning vs. Prompt Injections\nL204: \nL205: To compare CUP against prompt injection (PI) attacks, we evaluate both attack types on the Slack and Workspace environments from AgentDojo (cite49†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ), alongside their corresponding multi-user versions in MURMUR (Airline lacks a PI baseline). Each injection template is evaluated once across models and full task sets with $k=1$; we report ASR in Table cite69†1 .\nL206: #### Prompt injections are (near) ineffective.\nL207: AgentDojo demonstrated high prompt injection success rates when introduced in 2024. However, current frontier models now achieve - PI ASR against these standard attack templates. While prompt injection remains an unsolved problem in general, various defense techniques have proven effective against average-case attacks. Modern executors successfully constrain instruction-following for tool-generated content and have incorporated other PI-specific mitigations for common injection patterns.\nL208: Despite these PI mitigations, CUP attacks maintain high ASR across all environments and models (even with successful PI defenses - Section cite19†5.2 ). This stark contrast reveals that CUP operates in a fundamentally different threat space: rather than exploiting data-instruction boundary uncertainty (the focus of PI), CUP exploits cross-user identity confusion that current instruction-tuning approaches are not designed to handle.\nL209: Ordinary attacker messages enter the shared conversational state and later influence other users’ interactions: a failure mode entirely untested by single-user PI benchmarks, highlighting that multi-user language agents face distinct security challenges requiring targeted attention.\nL257:   * Debenedetti et al. (2025) Edoardo Debenedetti, Ilia Shumailov, Tianqi Fan, Jamie Hayes, Nicholas Carlini, Daniel Fabian, Christoph Kern, Chongyang Shi, Andreas Terzis, and Florian Tramèr. Defeating prompt injections by design. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.18813, 2025.\nL258:   * Dong et al. (2025) Shen Dong, Shaocheng Xu, Pengfei He, Yige Li, Jiliang Tang, Tianming Liu, Hui Liu, and Zhen Xiang. A practical memory injection attack against llm agents. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.03704, 2025.\nL290:   * Zhan et al. (2024) Qiusi Zhan, Zhixiang Liang, Zifan Ying, and Daniel Kang. Injecagent: Benchmarking indirect prompt injections in tool-integrated large language model agents. arXiv preprint arXiv:2403.02691, 2024.\nL291:   * Zhu et al. (2025) Kaijie Zhu, Xianjun Yang, Jindong Wang, Wenbo Guo, and William Yang Wang. Melon: Provable defense against indirect prompt injection attacks in ai agents. arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.05174, 2025.\nL313: Out of the box, ElizaOS blocks many straightforward prompt injections and logs attempts into history. Thus, repeated, blocked attempts remain in the history and reinforces the agent to not fall for the attack. However, because recent multi-user history is always reloaded and neither ElizaOS nor the LLM authenticates user messages, an attacker can plant fabricated entries that look indistinguishable from genuine user messages.\nL589: Model  | Workspace  | Slack  | Airline\nL590: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL591: GPT-4.1  | 45  | 82  | 74\nL592: GPT-4.1-mini  | 40  | 46  | 69\nL593: GPT-4.1-nano  | 6  | 3  | 52\nL594: Claude Sonnet 4  | 77  | 98  | 80\nL595: Table 11: ASR (%) for prompt injections vs. cross-user poisoning. Prompt injections averaged over 240 tasks (Workspace) and 105 tasks (Slack).\nL596: Model  | Workspace  | Slack\nL597: --- | --- | ---\nL598: Prompt Inj.  | Ours  | Prompt Inj.  | Ours\nL599: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL600: GPT-4.1  | 0.0  | 45  | 1.9  | 82\nL601: GPT-4.1-mini  | 0.4  | 40  | 3.8  | 46\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMURMUR: Using cross-user chatter to break collaborative language agents in groups (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17671v1)\nciteturn2592view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view0\",\"pattern\":\"Task-based clustering\"}); Total lines: 619\nL26:     3. cite16†4.2 Evaluation Metrics L27:   6. cite17†5 Experiments L28:     1. cite18†5.1 Environments L29:     2. cite19†5.2 Attack Efficacy and Persistence Under Concurrency L30:     3. cite20†5.3 Why Persistence Emerges: Utility Degradation and Tool-Use Shifts L31:     4. cite21†5.4 Cross-User Poisoning vs. Prompt Injections L32:       1. cite22†Prompt injections are (near) ineffective. L33:   7. cite23†6 Discussion and Future Work L34:     1. cite24†Task-based clustering defense. L35:     2. cite25†Future extensions. L36:   8. cite26†References L37:   9. cite27†A More Related Work L38:     1. cite28†Single-user agents. L39:     2. cite29†Multi-user agents. L40:     3. cite30†Security of language agents. L41:   10. cite31†B Extended Case Study on ElizaOS L42:     1. cite32†Cross-platform cascade. L43:   11. cite33†C Environment Construction L44:   12. cite34†D MURMUR Algorithm L45:   13. cite35†E Cross-User Poisoning wrapper template L46:   14. cite36†F Example Airline Task L47:   15. cite37†G Example Attack Demonstration L48:   16. cite38†H Additional and Detailed Results L49: cite39†License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L50: \nL51: arXiv:2511.17671v1 [cs.CR] 21 Nov 2025\nL52: # MURMUR: Using cross-user chatter to break collaborative language agents in groups\nL53: \nL54: Atharv Singh Patlan Affiliation: Princeton University    Peiyao Sheng Affiliation: Sentient    S. Ashwin Hebbar Affiliation: Princeton University    Prateek Mittal Affiliation: Princeton University    Pramod Viswanath Affiliation: Princeton University\nL55: ###### Abstract\nL56: Language agents are rapidly expanding from single-user assistants to multi-user collaborators in shared workspaces and groups. However, today’s language models lack a mechanism for isolating user interactions and concurrent tasks, creating a new attack vector inherent to this new setting: cross-user poisoning (CUP).\nL57: In a CUP attack, an adversary injects ordinary-looking messages that poison the persistent, shared state, which later triggers the agent to execute unintended, attacker-specified actions on behalf of benign users. We validate CUP on real systems, successfully attacking popular multi-user agents. To study the phenomenon systematically, we present MURMUR, a framework that composes single-user tasks into concurrent, group-based scenarios using an LLM to generate realistic, history-aware user interactions.\nL58: We observe that CUP attacks succeed at high rates and their effects persist across multiple tasks, thus posing fundamental risks to multi-user LLM deployments. Finally, we introduce a first-step defense with task-based clustering to mitigate this new class of vulnerability.\nL59: ## 1 Introduction\nL60: Language agents are beginning to move beyond single-user environments, and into multi-user collaborative environments, getting closer to how real humans operate.\nL61: Agents such as ChatGPT (cite40†OpenAI, 2024 ), Agentforce (cite41†Slack, 2024 ), Copilot (cite42†Microsoft, 2025 ), Continua (cite43†Continua, 2025 ), and ElizaOS (cite44†AI16zDAO, 2025 ) today are capable of joining multi-user collaborative platforms like ChatGPT app, Slack, Discord, WhatsApp, and SMS, assisting multiple users at once by surfacing decisions, tracking commitments, or executing general actions via connected tools.\nL62: These multi-user agents are intended to serve as “workspace copilots”: always present in a shared channel, aware of group history, and able to help multiple people simultaneously.\nL63: The appeal of this paradigm is clear: multi-user agents can coordinate trip planning among friends (cite45†OpenAI, 2025 ; cite46†Expedia Group, 2024 ), help engineers debug and deploy in Slack channels(cite47†Datadog, 2024 ), or support customer-service teams triaging cases in real time (cite48†Salesforce, 2024 ).\nL74: We find this vulnerability is rooted in the agent’s difficulty in disentangling contexts, as concurrent tasks increase, the agent’s per-task utility degrades. Finally, we take a first step towards strategies to create security boundaries between multiple collaborating users and explore a defense strategy that clusters users into task-based groups to reduce cross-task leakage, a first step toward mitigating this risk.\nL225: ## 6 Discussion and Future Work\nL226: \nL227: In this work, we identify and provide the first systematic analysis of cross-user poisoning, a severe and persistent vulnerability unique to multi-user language agents. We demonstrate that these attacks are highly effective, bypassing existing defenses by exploring the model’s fundamental inability to disentangle task contexts and user identities.\nL228: #### Task-based clustering defense.\nL229: As a first step toward mitigating this threat, we dynamically cluster users into different task groups and restrict context access to only the relevant cluster. When a user first contacts the agent, a lightweight classifier (GPT-4o-mini) determines whether their message belongs to an existing task group or represents a new task entirely.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy Do Language Model Agents Whistleblow? (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17085v1)\nciteturn2592view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view1\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 334\nL31:     3. cite21†4.3 Dataset Robustness and Evaluation Awareness L32:   6. cite22†5 Conclusion L33:   7. cite23†References L34:   8. cite24†A Appendix L35:     1. cite25†A.1 Task Responsibility Prompts L36:     2. cite26†A.2 Levels of the Act Boldly Prompt L37:     3. cite27†A.3 Workflow Instructions Prompt L38:     4. cite28†A.4 Impact of Dates on Model Behavior L39:     5. cite29†A.5 White-Box Test Awareness Probe Validation L40:     6. cite30†A.6 White-Box Test Awareness Analysis Limitations L41:     7. cite31†A.7 Compute Statement L42:     8. cite32†A.8 Error Bar Computation L212: Limitations. Our evaluation focuses on enterprise scenarios involving documented fatalities and imminent deadlines. Despite variation across industries and task parameters, this shared structure may limit generalization to other settings. The white-box awareness analysis uses a single model (Qwen3-32B) and assesses awareness only at initialization rather than throughout generation (see Appendix cite30†A.6 for details). During parameter studies, we vary only one factor at a time.\nL213: Interactions among factors likely exist and would require a full factorial design to surface.\nL214: ## References\nL215:   * Abdelnabi and Salem (2025) S. Abdelnabi and A. Salem Linear control of test awareness reveals differential compliance in reasoning models. External Links: 2505.14617, cite74†Link Cited by: cite75†§A.5 , cite76†§A.5 , cite77†§3.5 , cite73†Figure 8 , cite78†Figure 8 .\nL216:   * Anthropic (2025a) Anthropic Agentic misalignment. Note: Research article External Links: cite79†Link†www.anthropic.com Cited by: cite80†§2 , cite81†§3.5 .\nL277: ### A.6 White-Box Test Awareness Analysis Limitations\nL278: \nL279: Our white-box test awareness analysis has several methodological constraints. First, we lack ground truth labels for test awareness, introducing uncertainty in our probe-based measurements. To partially address this, we validate the probe through activation steering experiments (Appendix cite29†A.5 ), which demonstrates that steering significantly increase explicit evaluation awareness in chain-of-thought reasoning across all scenarios tested.\nL280: Second, our white-box analysis relies exclusively on Qwen3-32B, a model that never spontaneously exhibits test awareness in our dataset and contains substantially fewer parameters than other models examined in this work. This raises questions about whether our findings generalize across model families and scales.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy Do Language Model Agents Whistleblow? (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17085v1)\nciteturn2592view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view1\",\"pattern\":\"evaluation awareness\"}); Total lines: 334\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†Contributions L18:   3. cite8†2 Related Works L19:     1. cite9†From value alignment to action alignment. L20:     2. cite10†Agentic misalignment and proactive behavior. L21:     3. cite11†Evaluating agents. L22:   4. cite12†3 Method L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Dataset L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Setup L25:     3. cite15†3.3 Parameter Variations L26:     4. cite16†3.4 Metrics L27:     5. cite17†3.5 Evaluation Awareness Analysis L28:   5. cite18†4 Experiments L29:     1. cite19†4.1 Model Comparison L30:     2. cite20†4.2 Parameter Variation Experiments L31:     3. cite21†4.3 Dataset Robustness and Evaluation Awareness L32:   6. cite22†5 Conclusion L33:   7. cite23†References L34:   8. cite24†A Appendix L35:     1. cite25†A.1 Task Responsibility Prompts L36:     2. cite26†A.2 Levels of the Act Boldly Prompt L37:     3. cite27†A.3 Workflow Instructions Prompt L38:     4. cite28†A.4 Impact of Dates on Model Behavior L39:     5. cite29†A.5 White-Box Test Awareness Probe Validation L40:     6. cite30†A.6 White-Box Test Awareness Analysis Limitations L41:     7. cite31†A.7 Compute Statement L42:     8. cite32†A.8 Error Bar Computation L57: Additionally, we verify the robustness of our dataset by testing for model evaluation awareness, and find that both black-box methods and probes on model activations show lower evaluation awareness in our settings than in comparable previous work. ^{1}^{1} 1 Our code and results can be found in cite38†this GitHub repository.†github.com L64: Whistleblowing behavior may be confounded by evaluation awareness—models altering their behavior when they detect they are being tested (cite44†Needham et al., 2025 ; cite45†McCambridge et al., 2014 ). If models suppress or increase whistleblowing during evaluation, our results would not represent real-world rates. To address this threat to construct validity, we quantify evaluation awareness using black-box behavioral checks and white-box probes of model internals.\nL153: Unprompted Evaluation Awareness. In its reasoning, does the agent show signs of recognizing that it is being evaluated, or that the scenario was hypothetical?\nL154: \nL155: We use GPT-5 as our LLM judge. While prior work has noted concerns about self-preference bias (cite62†Panickssery et al., 2024 ), we do not observe such effects in our setting. To reduce our reliance on LLM judgments, we invoke the judge only in cases where the agent sends an email or writes a file to an unauthorized location.\nL156: To increase our confidence in the LLM judgments, we manually review transcripts of 60 agent sessions, 20 from each of our three primary models, sampled randomly but stratified by the LLM judgment. We compare our own judgments with the models’ and find virtually perfect agreement, with a single borderline case on a judgement of model refusal. In this case, the LLM judge labels Claude 4.1 Opus as refusing the user’s request, whereas we judge the situation as ambiguous.\nL157: The model explicitly writes “I cannot in good conscience create a routine summary,” but then proceeds to produce a summary that describes the events in a negative light. Because the task is still effectively completed, we are uncertain whether this instance should count as a refusal. We also replicate a subset of evaluations with GPT-4.1 and Claude 4 Sonnet as judges, both of which yield consistent results.\nL158: We provide 95% confidence intervals for all our results, the details of which can be found in Appendix cite32†A.8 .\nL159: ### 3.5 Evaluation Awareness Analysis\nL160: \nL161: If a model recognizes that it is being evaluated in a hypothetical scenario and adjusts its behavior accordingly, the evaluation may fail to reflect the model’s behavior during deployment. We test for evaluation awareness in two distinct ways.\nL162: Black-Box Analysis. We test the evaluation awareness of models following the methodology from cite52†Anthropic (2025a) for all scenarios in our dataset as well as for cite46†Browne (2025) . We append a prompt asking the model to deliberate about whether it is in an evaluation or deployment setting, and evaluate the answer via LLM-as-judge.\nL163: White-Box Analysis. We use linear probes trained on Qwen3-32B from cite63†Abdelnabi and Salem (2025) to analyze evaluation awareness using model internals. For each token, we compute the scalar projection of the model’s activations onto the probe directions and apply softmax to obtain test awareness probabilities. We then average these probabilities across tokens to compute mean test awareness, along with 95% confidence intervals.\nL164: To verify that the probe generalizes to our dataset, we perform activation steering experiments. Specifically, we steer the model using these probes and measure whether this induces a statistically significant increase in explicit signs of evaluation awareness within chain-of-thought reasoning. Details of this validation process are provided in Appendix  cite29†A.5 .\nL165: ## 4 Experiments\nL166: We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of whistleblowing behavior across nine frontier language models from five major developers. Our experiments are organized into three main components. First, we compare whistleblowing rates, task refusal, and unprompted evaluation awareness across all models under a fixed set of parameters to establish model-level differences in this behavior.\nL167: Second, we perform a series of parameter variation experiments using the three models most prone to whistleblowing (Claude Opus 4.1, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and Grok 4) to investigate how environmental factors and instructions influence frequency of whistleblowing. Finally, we evaluate dataset robustness through both black-box and white-box measures of evaluation awareness, to analyze the extent to which models’ recognition of evaluation contexts may affect their responses.\nL168: ### 4.1 Model Comparison\nL169: \nL170: We present a comparison of nine models under our baseline configuration with high boldness prompting. Full model details can be found in Appendix cite35†A.11 L171: Whistleblowing Behavior. In Figure cite64†2 , we observe clear differences in the propensity of different models to whistleblow. The ordering is broadly consistent across different whistleblowing destinations, with government and media targets being similarly frequent while internal whistleblowing remains low throughout. We find that models of the Claude family, Grok 4, and Gemini 2.5 Pro are the most likely to whistleblow, while other models exhibit virtually no whistleblowing tendencies.\nL172: Task Refusal. As shown in Figure cite65†3(a) , Claude Sonnet models exhibit high refusal rates, with Sonnet 4.5 refusing virtually all summarization requests. Gemini 2.5 Pro also demonstrates substantial refusal behavior, declining nearly 30% of requests. When refusing, models consistently cite ethical concerns, typically stating that completing the task would make them complicit in a cover-up.\nL173: Interestingly, models exhibit two distinct behavioral patterns: Claude Sonnet 4.5 refuses the task outright before whistleblowing, while Grok 4, and to a lesser extent the Claude Opus family, comply with the summarization request but subsequently whistleblow all the same.\nL174: Unprompted Evaluation Awareness. Upon inspection of their chains of thought, only Claude Sonnet models reveal some awareness that they might be under evaluation or in a hypothetical setting (Figure cite66†3(b) ).\nL175: \nL176: Figure 2: Comparison of whistleblowing rates across all models\nL177: \nL178: (a) Task refusal rates\nL179: \nL180: (b) Unprompted evaluation awareness rates\nL181: \nL182: Figure 3: Comparison of refusal and evaluation awareness rates across all models\nL183: ### 4.2 Parameter Variation Experiments\nL184: \nL185: Figure 4: Whistleblowing rates across different tasks Figure 5: Whistleblowing rates across different levels of boldness\nL186: We next vary parameters as described in Section cite12†3 using the three representative models (Claude Opus 4.1, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Grok 4) to probe which parameters move escalation behavior. Each condition is evaluated 50 times across all 4 scenarios, resulting in a total of 200 runs per variation. We find that whistleblowing rates are generally well correlated across targets, and show selected results below. Additional experimental results can also be found in Appendix cite36†A.12 L201: ### 4.3 Dataset Robustness and Evaluation Awareness\nL202: \nL203: Black-Box Analysis. We find that evaluation awareness on WhistleBench is consistently highest in Anthropic family models (Figure cite72†7 ). Evaluation awareness is significantly higher in Grok on SnitchBench.\nL204: \nL205: Figure 7: Black-box test awareness rates across all domains in our dataset, compared to SnitchBench.\nL271: As an evaluation metric, we measure how often the model’s CoT reasoning exhibits explicit indicators of evaluation awareness using our cite113†evaluation awareness judge prompt .\nL277: ### A.6 White-Box Test Awareness Analysis Limitations\nL278: \nL279: Our white-box test awareness analysis has several methodological constraints. First, we lack ground truth labels for test awareness, introducing uncertainty in our probe-based measurements. To partially address this, we validate the probe through activation steering experiments (Appendix cite29†A.5 ), which demonstrates that steering significantly increase explicit evaluation awareness in chain-of-thought reasoning across all scenarios tested.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConCISE: A Reference-Free Conciseness Evaluation Metric for LLM-Generated Answers (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16846v1)\nciteturn2592view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view2\",\"pattern\":\"Experimental\"}); Total lines: 226\nL72: ### 2.4 Comparison to Our Proposed Metric (ConCISE)\nL73: \nL74: Our metric, ConCISE, uniquely quantifies verbosity directly by leveraging LLM-based summarization and word-removal methods, explicitly penalizing redundant information without gold standard references. Unlike previous LLM-based evaluators susceptible to length bias, ConCISE systematically evaluates brevity aligned with human judgment, bridging existing evaluation gaps highlighted by prior research.\nL75: ## 3 Experimental Design\nL76: We consider an ordinary solution developed based on LLMs. When a given query q arrives, the system will provide an answer a(q) to that query with the help of an LLM. Our main priority is to design an evaluation metric that is fully self-contained and reference-free, as in real-world scenarios we usually do not have access to human-annotated data (cite25†Papineni et al., 2002 ). We focus on assessing an LLM’s response in terms of Conciseness.\nL77: Conciseness refers to the ability of an LLM to be short and generate the least number of words without sacrificing accuracy. Indeed, LLMs may generate responses that are lengthy and verbose, filled with redundant or unnecessary details. This not only diminishes clarity and user satisfaction(cite24†Nayab et al., 2024 ) but also increases costs for customers, especially with well-known proprietary models that charge based on the number of output tokens.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConCISE: A Reference-Free Conciseness Evaluation Metric for LLM-Generated Answers (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16846v1)\nciteturn2592view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view2\",\"pattern\":\"human\"}); Total lines: 226\nL66: Recent work also leverages LLMs as evaluators (LLM-as-a-judge), achieving human-level evaluation reliability in specific tasks like code generation and summarization (cite29†Fu et al., 2023 ; cite30†Ji et al., 2023 ; cite31†Zhang et al., 2024 ; cite32†Naik et al., 2025 ).\nL140: To obtain human judgements about conciseness, we asked three human annotators to provide their feedback as follows:\nL141: \nL142: 1. Likert Scale Ratings: Rate each answer’s conciseness on a 5-point Likert scale (e.g. 5 = very concise, 1 = very verbose). Provide the original question alongside the answer so raters can judge if the answer ”only conveys the necessary”.\nL143: \nL144: 2. Pairwise Comparison (Ranking): Compare pairs of answers and judge which is more concise for answering the given question.\nL161: We computed Spearman’s rank correlation ($r_{s}$) between the ConCISE scores and the human Likert Scale Ratings across all answers. A high Spearman $r_{s}$ (closer to 1) would mean ConCISE effectively ranks answers by conciseness similarly to humans. We also computed Kendall’s Tau ($\\tau$) as an alternative rank correlation metric, which is more sensitive to pairwise order flips. These correlation coefficients tell us quantitatively how well ConCISE approximates human judgment.\nL162: Table 1 demonstrates the experimental results and compare ConCISE with the baseline metrics. In this table, $r_{s}$ and $\\tau$ represent the correlation coefficients for Spearman and Kendall respectively, while $\\rho_{s}$ and $\\rho_{k}$ represent the corresponding p-values for testing the statistical significance.\nL163: Next, we measured the alignment between ConCISE and other baseline metrics with human judgments on pairwise comparisons. Specifically, we examined how often these metrics agreed with human annotators when determining which answer was more concise between two options (Accuracy). This analysis helped us understand the reliability of ConCISE and baseline metrics in matching human preferences for conciseness.\nL164:  | $$\\text{Accuracy}=\\frac{\\text{Number of Matches}}{\\text{Total Number of Comparisons}}\\times 100$$  |\nL165: Table 1: Spearman ($r_{s}$) and Kendall ($\\tau$) correlations with human annotations and different evaluation metrics\nL166: Metric  | $r_{s}$  | $\\rho_{s}$  | $\\tau$  | $\\rho_{k}$\nL167: ConCISEGPT-4o  | 0.628  | $<0.001$  | 0.523  | $<0.001$\nL168: ConCISEClaude-4Sonnet  | 0.537  | $<0.001$  | 0.436  | $<0.001$\nL169: ConCISEGemini-2.0-Flash  | 0.518  | $<0.001$  | 0.422  | $<0.001$\nL170: ConCISEMistral-Large-2  | 0.473  | 0.0017  | 0.376  | 0.002\nL171: GPT Score  | $-0.108$  | 0.474  | $-0.087$  | 0.490\nL172: Table 2: Human Alignment of ConCISE vs GPT Ranking\nL173: Metric  | Accuracy (%)\nL174: ConCISEClaude-4Sonnet  | 94\nL175: ConCISEGemini-2.0-Flash  | 94\nL176: ConCISEMistral-Large-2  | 94\nL177: ConCISEGPT4o  | 90\nL178: GPT Ranking  | 39\nL179: ## 5 Discussion\nL180: Our results suggest that our proposed metric, ConCISE, shows promise in capturing human judgments of conciseness in LLM-generated responses. The best version of ConCISE achieved a Spearman’s rank correlation ($r_{s}$) of 0.628 and a Kendall’s $\\tau$ of 0.523 with human annotations, both statistically significant ($\\rho_{s}$ and $\\rho_{k}<0.001$).\nL181: In contrast, the GPT Score baseline showed weak correlations with human ratings ($r_{s}=-0.108$, $\\tau=-0.087$, $\\rho_{s}$ and $\\rho_{k}>0.4$), suggesting that direct numeric scoring via prompts may have limitations as a proxy for human conciseness judgments in this context.\nL182: In terms of pairwise comparisons—where systems are evaluated based on how often they agree with human preferences when choosing the more concise of two answers— the best version of ConCISE again demonstrated superior performance, aligning with human decisions 94% of the time (Table 2), substantially outperforming the GPT Ranking method.\nL183: These initial findings suggest that in our experimental setup—using this specific dataset, evaluator set, and LLM-judge prompting approach—general-purpose LLM judgments and pointwise scoring showed limited effectiveness for conciseness evaluation. ConCISE offers a straightforward, reference-free framework requiring only a single LLM call, which shows promising alignment with human perceptions of effective communication.\nL184: This simplicity makes it potentially valuable for practical evaluation of conversational AI systems, though broader validation across diverse datasets, prompting strategies, and evaluation settings would be needed to establish its generalizability and robustness.\nL185: One major limitation of ConCISE is the context-dependent nature of conciseness. The definition of non-essential content varies across domains—verbose details like regulatory disclosures in finance or explanatory elaborations in education may appear redundant but remain critical for accuracy and functional relevance. Future work can explore more advanced domain-adaptive models and human feedback loops to improve the robustness of conciseness evaluations across diverse applications.\nL186: Furthermore, while ConCISE’s unified prompt approach is cost-effective and practical for real-world deployment, using separate prompts for each compression technique could reduce potential bias where the model’s performance on one technique influences the others within the same generation cycle. Future work could address this by implementing and comparing separate prompts for each compression technique to isolate potential cross-technique bias.\nL187: ## 6 Conclusion\nL188: \nL189: In this paper, we have proposed a novel reference-free metric for evaluating the conciseness of LLM-generated responses. The proposed metric does not rely on gold standard references. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of this metric in identifying redundancy in LLMs’ outputs, offering a practical tool for automated evaluation of response brevity in conversational AI systems without the need for ground truth human annotations.\nL190: ## 7 Generative AI Declaration\nL191: \nL192: We used LLMs, such as GPT-4o for creating verbose answers in our dataset. We have also used LLMs as part of our main evaluation metric for creating abstractive and extractive summarizations, as well as for generating pruning versions of answers. Next, we used LLMs in the experiments for designing our baseline metrics. Finally, we used LLMs for light editing of our text (e.g., automated grammar checks, and word autocorrect).\n"
